course: world
lesson: 63
title: Soil and what lives in it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Soil is the thin, loose top layer of the land where plants grow. It is a mix of tiny bits of broken rock, humus (dark rotted remains of plants and animals), water, air, and a huge number of living things.
explain: Soil forms very slowly: weathering breaks rock into sand, silt and clay, and dead leaves, roots and animals rot into humus; making just a few centimetres of good soil can take hundreds of years.
explain: Soil is alive: one handful of good soil holds more tiny living things than there are people on Earth. Earthworms, beetles, woodlice, fungi and bacteria break down dead matter and give its nutrients back to the soil for plants; these are the decomposers.
explain: Earthworms are the gardeners of the soil: they eat dead leaves and soil, leave behind rich droppings called casts, and dig tunnels that let air and water reach the roots.
explain: Soils differ: sandy soil has big grains with big gaps, so water drains through it fast; clay soil has tiny grains packed close, so it holds water and can become sticky and waterlogged; loam, a mix of sand, silt, clay and humus, is the best for most crops.
fact: Soil is made of broken rock, humus, water, air and living things.
fact: Humus is the dark material made from rotted plants and animals, and it is rich in nutrients.
fact: It can take hundreds of years to form a few centimetres of soil.
fact: Decomposers such as fungi, bacteria and earthworms break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil.
fact: Earthworm tunnels let air and water into the soil.
fact: Sandy soil drains water quickly; clay soil holds water and drains slowly.
fact: Loam is a mix of sand, silt, clay and humus and is good for most plants.
fact: Topsoil is the dark upper layer with the most humus and life; below it lie subsoil and then solid rock.
fact: Plant roots hold soil in place, so bare soil is easily washed or blown away; this loss is called soil erosion.
fact: A compost heap turns kitchen and garden waste into humus with the help of decomposers.
exercise: What is soil made of? | Broken rock, humus, water, air and living things | Only sand and water mixed together | Crushed stones and nothing else | Dead leaves pressed into a hard layer on top of the rock
exercise: What is humus? | Dark material from rotted plants and animals | A kind of small grey stone found deep in the ground | Water trapped in little pockets under the ground | A type of worm that lives in the soil and eats it
exercise: How long does it take for a few centimetres of soil to form? | Hundreds of years | A few days | One summer | A single rainy week
exercise: What do decomposers do? | Break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil | Eat living plants until they die | Make their own food from sunlight, just like green leaves do | Turn soil back into hard rock
exercise: Which of these is a decomposer? | A fungus | A sunflower | A rabbit | A hawk
exercise: How do earthworms help plants? | Their tunnels let in air and water, and their casts add nutrients | They eat the insects that harm the roots | They carry seeds deep under the ground | They give off light that helps roots grow
exercise: Why does water drain quickly through sandy soil? | Its big grains leave big gaps between them | Sand soaks up and stores all the water | Sand is warmer, so the water dries at once | Sand pushes water back up to the surface
exercise: Why can clay soil become waterlogged? | Its tiny grains are packed close and water drains slowly | Clay has big gaps between its grains that fill with water | Clay makes extra water of its own out of the damp air around it | Clay is always found near rivers, so it is always soaked through
exercise: Which soil is best for most crops? | Loam | Pure sand | Pure clay | Gravel
exercise: Which layer of soil has the most humus and life? | Topsoil | Subsoil | Solid rock | Bedrock sand
exercise: What is soil erosion? | The loss of soil washed or blown away | The slow forming of new soil from rock | The rotting of fallen leaves into humus | The digging of tunnels by worms and moles
exercise: Why do plant roots protect the soil? | They hold the soil in place against wind and rain | They make the soil heavier than rock so it cannot move | They stop all rain from reaching it | They turn the soil into clay
exercise: What happens in a compost heap? | Decomposers turn waste into humus | The waste turns into new plastic | The heat melts the waste into soil | Worms change the waste into stones
test: A gardener pours a jug of water on two pots. The water runs out of one pot almost at once but stays on top of the other for a long time. Which pot has clay soil? | The one where the water stays on top | The one where the water runs out at once | Both have clay soil | Neither, because clay lets water through fastest
test: Which is true about a forest floor? | Fallen leaves are broken down by fungi, bacteria and small animals into humus | Fallen leaves stay there forever because nothing eats them | The leaves are all blown away by the wind each winter | The trees take the dead leaves back into their trunks
test: A farmer ploughs a field on a slope and leaves it bare all winter. What is most likely to happen? | Rain washes much of the topsoil away | The soil grows thicker by spring | Worms move to the field from far away | The soil turns into loam on its own
test: If all the decomposers in a wood disappeared, what would happen? | Dead leaves and bodies would pile up and the soil would lose its nutrients | The trees would grow faster than ever | The soil would become richer and richer every year as the dead things piled up | Nothing would change at all
test: Why is it a problem when a flood carries away a field's topsoil? | It took hundreds of years to form and holds most of the nutrients | Topsoil is easy to make again in a week | The subsoil below is much richer anyway | Plants grow only in the rock below
test: A child digs a deep hole in a garden. The soil at the top is dark, lower down it is paler and stonier, and at the bottom there is rock. Why is the top darkest? | It holds the most humus from rotted plants | It is wetter from all the rain that falls on it | The sunlight has burned it dark over the years | It is the oldest layer of all, so it is darkest
test: Which one does the most to keep soil airy and well drained in a garden? | Earthworms digging tunnels | Snails eating leaves | Birds pulling up worms to eat | Bees visiting flowers
test: A pupil puts banana peel and a plastic bag in a compost heap. After some months, what will he find? | The peel has rotted away but the bag is still there | Both have rotted away into humus | The bag has rotted but the peel is still there | Both are exactly as they were
test: why's there worms everywhere after rain, are they even good for the soil | Yes, their tunnels let air and water into the soil | No, they eat the plant roots and harm the soil badly | No, they make the soil too hard to dig | Yes, but only because they eat the weeds
test: our garden's clay and it stays soggy for ages, why | Clay soil holds water and drains slowly | Clay soil has no air in it at all, only water | Clay soil soaks up rain straight from the air | Clay soil is always below freezing in winter
test: my sister says soil is just crushed up rock and nothing else. right? | No, soil also has humus, water, air and living things | Yes, soil is only broken rock and nothing more | Yes, but only the soil under the topsoil | No, soil is mostly made of old leaves only
test: Sam reckons if u dig up the plants the soil stays put no problem. true? | No, bare soil is easily washed or blown away without roots | Yes, soil never moves once it has formed | Yes, plant roots actually loosen soil and help it wash away | No, the soil would turn straight into solid rock
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises humus? | Dark material formed from rotted plants and animals, rich in nutrients | Small fragments of rock without any nutrients | The solid rock beneath the subsoil | Water held between grains of clay
exam: Identify the function performed by decomposers such as fungi, bacteria and earthworms. | They break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil | They manufacture soil from solid rock within a single day | They remove all air and water from the soil | They consume the roots of living plants exclusively and nothing else
exam: It is given that one soil drains rapidly whilst another retains water and drains slowly. Determine the respective designations of these soils. | Sandy soil and clay soil respectively | Clay soil and sandy soil respectively | Loam and subsoil respectively | Subsoil and topsoil respectively
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the cause of soil erosion. | Bare soil without roots is readily washed or blown away | Earthworm tunnels cause the soil to collapse | Humus causes the soil to dissolve in rain | Plant roots loosen the soil and carry it off
